WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:06.390
I've been warning you about today for months,
July 30th, the day that we knew we would get

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:10.380
our estimate of the second quarter GDP number
of the United States.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.009
We received that number this morning.

00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:13.009
And it is historic.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:14.040
It's historically bad.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.320
I mean, why did Donald Trump tweet today that
he wants to delay the election?

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.890
I believe that the GDP number is part of it.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:27.380
In addition to the collapsing polls, the United
States economy shrank by the biggest amount

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.700
on record in the second quarter.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:38.390
This year, annualized second quarter GDP was
down thirty two point nine percent.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.440
Thirty two point.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.470
The economy contracted annualized by roughly
a third in the second quarter.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.150
Now the expectation was to be down 34.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:57.079
So being down 33 is like slightly less bad
than the expectation, but a historic, disastrous

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:58.079
number.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:00:59.079
Now we know why.

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:02.060
It's very easy to say, listen, it's not Trump's
fault, it's coronavirus.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:07.190
OK, but the Trump administration has failed
in terms of the corona virus response.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:13.070
So the health numbers, meaning cases and deaths,
are worse than the numbers had to be.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:17.650
Just look at other countries and the economic
numbers are also worse than they had to be.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:22.670
The big areas of reduction in GDP were not
shockingly consumer spending and the personal

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.299
consumption category.

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:26.240
And remember that this is just an estimate.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.060
We get more precise numbers about another
month out.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.689
So this is an estimate on July 30th.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:36.159
We're expecting the sort of more more fine
tuned numbers right around August 30th.

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:39.610
And who knows what will be going on by then?

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:40.650
With coronavirus.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.920
Now, meanwhile, new jobless claims have also
gone in the wrong direction.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:52.750
New jobless claims are up for the second week
in a row to one point forty three million

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:53.970
new claims.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:02:00.090
This is the 19th straight week with more than
one million people losing their jobs.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:06.520
Of the 19 weeks new jobless claims, it was
like six point six million that first week

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:07.900
or the second week.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.739
And then they've been declining for 15 weeks.

00:02:10.739 --> 00:02:12.300
Now things have turned around.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.610
And new jobless claims have started to increase.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:20.790
And the unfortunate reality is that it could
get much worse if states have to keep continue

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.099
pulling back.

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:24.880
If we continue to see cases rise.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.850
Deaths have spiked, then we'll talk about
that next.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:32.330
I believe states are going to have to pull
back and the numbers are going to once again

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.330
get worse.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:38.400
We were we were told in April, May, by June
1st, June 15th, there will be very few new

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.230
cases and barely any deaths.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.880
There will be a break between wave one and
what is expected to be a second wave in the

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:44.880
fall.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:45.950
We're not getting that break.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:52.800
We are actually when we got down to 20000
new cases a day, things started opening irresponsibly

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.030
in some states, more responsibly in others.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:57.800
And we are now seeing the effects of that.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.069
And the next thing I want to talk about is
the numbers and the trajectory that these

00:03:02.069 --> 00:04:28.430
numbers could very soon take.

